            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: 60% to Meta in Cold Start is Reasonable, But Only If Creatives Deliver</h2>
<p>There's no universal formula for budget allocation in cross-border independent websites, but there's a logical framework worth following. Allocating 60% to Meta during the cold-start phase is relatively safe for testing purposes, with remaining budget going to TikTok and Google Discovery for incremental audience coverage. This ratio isn't fixed—it's based on two facts: Meta's conversion tracking is mature, and the first 100 orders generate the most valuable user profiling data; TikTok traffic is cheaper but has a longer conversion path, better suited for scaling once your site has baseline conversion data.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The core principle of independent website ad budget allocation: verify the "traffic-to-conversion" loop at minimum cost first, then scale.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Three-Phase Budget Allocation Logic</h2>
<h3>Phase 1 (Days 0-30): Concentrated Testing, Fast Iteration</h3>
<p>The core mission here is finding ad groups and audience packages that generate orders. Recommended split: Meta 65%, TikTok 25%, Google 10%. The focus isn't how much to spend, but running 3-5 ad combinations with CPO below target.</p>
<p>A common operational mistake: teams dump 80% of budget into a single audience package, test for half a month, then discover this package only works for one specific creative style. When switching creatives, performance drops sharply. The correct approach is testing at least 3 audience dimensions per campaign—age groups, interest tags, and Custom Audiences.</p>
<h3>Phase 2 (Days 30-90): Data-Driven, Dynamic Adjustment</h3>
<p>Once effective ad groups are identified, budget allocation shifts from "even distribution" to "concentrated firepower." If an ad group maintains CPO at 80% or below target for 7 consecutive days, increase that channel's budget to 50%+. Start expanding secondary channels with small budgets testing TikTok Shopping and Google Performance Max.</p>
<p>A tool-category independent website's Phase 2 approach: Meta 55% (with 70% concentrated on top 2 ad groups), TikTok 30% (testing short video creatives and product promotion), Google 15% (search ads capturing brand terms). Within three months, daily orders grew from 15 to 80, with CPO down 42%.</p>
<h3>Phase 3 (Day 90+): Channel Diversification, Risk Priority</h3>
<p>Mature independent websites shouldn't rely on a single channel. The ideal state: Meta 40%, TikTok 30%, Google 20%, SEO/email marketing 10%. This phase requires considering two risks: platform policy changes causing account fluctuations, and creative fatigue driving down CTR.</p>
<h2>Three Often-Ignored Issues in Budget Allocation</h2>
<p><strong>1. Recharge Rhythm Matters More Than Total Amount</strong></p>
<p>Many teams focus on "how much to spend this month" but overlook recharge timing. Insufficient Meta account balance reduces bidding priority, and running out of funds during peak hours in Europe and America is the most common rookie mistake. Set balance warning lines and trigger auto-recharge notifications when balance drops below 20%.</p>
<p><strong>2. Attribution Must Be Calibrated Per Channel</strong></p>
<p>If running both Meta and TikTok but only tracking overall site ROAS, you'll make wrong decisions. The correct approach: set independent tracking parameters for each channel—Meta uses Pixel, TikTok uses TikTok Pixel, Google uses Conversion Tag. A home goods independent website operator shared: they discovered 60% of Google conversions were follow-up actions from users initially reached by Meta ads. After cutting Google budget, overall ROAS actually declined.</p>
<p><strong>3. Account Structure Affects Budget Efficiency More Than Creatives</strong></p>
<p>Budget allocation isn't just about money—it's about account architecture. Set separate campaigns per channel to avoid cross-channel competition. Within campaigns, organize ad groups by audience or creative, testing 2-3 creatives per group. Don't stuff 10 creatives into one group hoping the system auto-optimizes.</p>
<h2>Recommendations for Operations Teams</h2>
<p>Budget allocation is dynamic—don't set it and forget. Review each channel's CPO and ROAS weekly, making 3%-5% micro-adjustments based on data. Don't expect major actions daily; stable optimization rhythm is more effective than frequent changes. Additionally, cold-start phases often trap teams in anxiety over "waiting until data is sufficient before testing other channels." Instead of spending two months perfecting Meta, test TikTok in parallel—the audience overlap between the two platforms is far lower than you'd expect.</p>
